Stage 1 — Data Preparation
Goal: Convert raw CSVs into verified CoT training triples


data/raw/train.csv  (9,500 rows: id, prompt, answer)
        ↓
1a. Categorize examples by rule type
    (bit-ops, cipher, numeric, unit-conversion, physics, symbol)
        ↓
1b. Generate CoT rationales via teacher model (Claude/GPT-4)
    prompt → "think step by step → Answer: X"
        ↓
1c. Verify: keep only rationales where extracted answer == ground truth
    Target: ~7,500–8,000 verified triples (~80% pass rate)
        ↓
1d. Stratified split (by category)
    Train: 85%  →  data/splits/train_cot.jsonl
    Val:   15%  →  data/splits/val_cot.jsonl

data/raw/test.csv  (3 rows, no labels) → held for final submission
Stage 2 — Tokenization & Dataset Pipeline
Goal: Convert JSONL triples into model-ready tensors


2a. Choose prompt template and tokenize
    ### Problem: {prompt}
    ### Reasoning: {cot}
    ### Answer: {answer}

2b. Apply loss masking
    Labels[:prompt_end] = -100   ← mask question tokens
    Labels[prompt_end:] = token_ids  ← train on CoT + answer

2c. Verify max_seq_len distribution
    (your prompts avg 301 chars → 512 tokens is sufficient)

2d. Build DataLoader with dynamic padding
Stage 3 — Model Setup
Goal: Configure base model + LoRA for efficient fine-tuning


3a. Select base model
    Recommended: Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct or Phi-3-mini

3b. Load in 4-bit (QLoRA) to fit on single GPU

3c. Attach LoRA adapters
    target_modules: [q_proj, v_proj]
    rank: 16
    alpha: 32
    dropout: 0.05

3d. Verify trainable params (~1–2% of total)
Stage 4 — SFT Training (Teacher Distillation)
Goal: Train model to generate correct CoT + answer


4a. Configure training
    lr: 2e-4, epochs: 3–5
    batch: 8, grad_accum: 4 (effective batch 32)
    scheduler: cosine with warmup (100 steps)

4b. Training loop
    for each batch:
        forward pass (full sequence)
        loss on CoT + answer tokens only
        backward + optimizer step

4c. Checkpoint every 500 steps

4d. Track metrics per epoch
    - Training loss
    - Val loss
    - Val exact-match accuracy (by category)
Stage 5 — Evaluation & Error Analysis
Goal: Understand where the model fails before Stage 6


5a. Run inference on val set (greedy decoding)
    max_new_tokens: 256
    stop at <EOS>

5b. Extract answer from "### Answer:" section

5c. Compute metrics
    - Overall exact match accuracy
    - Per-category accuracy breakdown
    - Failure case samples per category

5d. Identify weakest category → inform Stage 6
Stage 6 — GRPO Self-Improvement (Optional Stage 2 RL)
Goal: Push accuracy further using the SFT model as starting point


6a. Use SFT checkpoint as initial policy

6b. For each prompt, sample N=8 responses

6c. Score each response
    reward = 1 if extracted_answer == ground_truth else 0

6d. GRPO update
    advantage = (reward - mean_reward) / std_reward
    policy gradient update using group-relative advantage

6e. Repeat for weakest categories first

6f. Stop when val accuracy plateaus
Stage 7 — Final Inference on Test Set
Goal: Generate predictions for test.csv submission


7a. Load best checkpoint (highest val exact-match)

8b. Run inference on test.csv (3 rows)
    Generate full CoT + answer

7c. Extract final answers

7d. Write outputs/results/submission.csv
    id, answer
Stage 8 — Experiment Tracking & Reproducibility
Goal: Keep everything reproducible across runs


8a. Log all runs: loss, accuracy, config → outputs/logs/
8b. Save rationale generation config (model used, temperature, prompt)
8c. Seed everything (torch, numpy, random) for reproducibility
8d. Save LoRA adapter separately from base model weights
Summary Timeline

Stage 1  →  Data + CoT generation    (most time: API calls for 9,500 rows)
Stage 2  →  Tokenization pipeline     (1 day)
Stage 3  →  Model + LoRA setup        (half day)
Stage 4  →  SFT training              (2–4 hours on single GPU)
Stage 5  →  Evaluation                (1–2 hours)
Stage 6  →  GRPO (optional)           (4–8 hours)
Stage 7  →  Test inference            (minutes)
Stage 8  →  Tracking (runs parallel to all stages)